{0 Affect concurrency model}

Affect provides parallel asynchronous functions and first-class
synchronous actions to orchestrate them.

An asynchronous function is a regular OCaml function. It only differs
in how it is called and how it returns its result. We use the term
asynchronous function for what is often an asynchronous
{e function call}.

Here is the model in a nutshell:

{ol
{- Asynchronous functions are structured on function scopes.

   If a function makes an {{!Affect.Fun.Async.call}asynchronous
   function call}, the call is scheduled concurrently and the caller
   continues immediately but when it reaches the end of its body
   it does not return before the asynchronous call does.}

{- Asynchronous functions are oblivious of their scheduling.

   No distinction between concurrency and parallelism can be made.
   Execution can be serialized or parallelized on an arbitrary number
   of domains depending on how the main program configures the
   system. Except for requests to be executed only on the main domain
   of the scheduler and prioritization hints, no assumption can be
   made on how they are scheduled by the running program. }

{- Asynchronous functions have {{!Affect.Fun.Async.Schedule.type-priority}
   priorities}.

   By default, asynchronous functions inherit the priority of their
   caller. Priorities are scheduling {e hints}, they can be ignored or
   dynamically changed by the scheduler. For example to avoid priority
   inversion when completion of a low priority call blocks a high
   priority call.}

{- Asynchronous functions are cooperatively scheduled.

   They must progress. If they are unable to do so they must either
   {{!Affect.Fun.Async.yield}yield} control or block by
   invoking a {{!Affect.Action.basics}synchronous action}. The
   function execution resumes once it is rescheduled or the action
   invocation synchronizes. Libraries can devise synchronous actions for
   asynchronous primitives and invoke them in regular functions to provide
   direct-style, seemingly blocking functions.}

{- Asynchronous functions have a {{!Affect.Fun.Async.Call_handler}call
   handler}.

   The call handler wraps the asynchronous function call and is
   propagated and composed with the call handler of descendent
   asynchronous functions calls. It can be used to setup effect
   handlers so that effect handling is structured on function
   scopes. The handler however must be synchronization safe as it can
   be invoked in parallel.}

{- The return value of an asynchronous function call is obtained by invoking
   a synchronous action.

   Each asynchronous function has {{!Affect.Fun.Async.get'}an action}
   which when invoked synchronizes whenever the function's return value or
   exception is available.}

{- Asynchronous functions can be {{!Affect.Fun.Async.section-cancelling}
   marked} for cancellation.

   A cancellation mark is permanent and cannot be reverted.}

{- Asynchronous function cancellation is structured on function scopes.

   When an asynchronous function is marked as cancelled, its own
   current and future asynchronous calls are also immediately marked
   as cancelled.}

{- Asynchronous function cancellation disables synchronous actions.

   If an asynchronous function is blocked on a synchronous action or
   tries to invoke one, it immediately synchronizes by raising the
   {{!Affect.Fun.Async.Cancelled}[Cancelled]} exception unless
   the invocation is made in an explicit scope that
   {{!Affect.Fun.Async.masking}masks} the
   cancellation.}

{- Asynchronous function cancellation is cooperative.

   An asynchronous function gets notified by an action invocation that
   it was cancelled however if it doesn't invoke actions it's up to
   function to {{!Affect.Fun.Async.checking}check} for
   cancellation. It is also up to the function to decide what it wants
   to do about the cancellation. For example it can simply return a
   partially computed result. If that doesn't make sense it is
   recommended to terminate the function as quickly as possible by
   releasing the resources it holds and return by raising
   {{!Affect.Fun.Async.Cancelled}[Cancelled]}. This compose well
   with the behaviour of actions on cancellation and can be naturally
   achieved by following the [Fun.protect] and
   {{!Affect.Fun.Async.protect}[Fun.Async.protect]} discipline.}  }

Some justifications about these decisions can be found in the
{{!page-design}design notes}.